+Adding General Solvent Mixtures
+*******************************
+
+To add an arbitrary solvent mixture around a model, call :meth:`addMixture` with
+a list of *components* specifying the contents of the mixture.  Each component
+must be a dictionary with the following keys:
+
+* ``topology``: A :class:`Topology` for the component.
+* ``positions``: Positions to go along with the topology.
+* ``count``: The number of copies of the component to insert.
+
+For example:
+::
+
+    component1 = PDBFile("component1.pdb")
+    component2 = PDBFile("component2.pdb")
+    modeller.addSolvent([
+        dict(topology=component1.topology, positions=component1.positions, count=1000),
+        dict(topology=component2.topology, positions=component2.positions, count=2000),
+    ])
+
+Each component could correspond to one type of molecule in the desired mixture,
+with each topology (and positions) representing a single molecule of each type.
+However, this is not required; you can just as easily add dimers or other small
+clusters of molecules.
+
+:meth:`addMixture` supports several of the options that :meth:`addSolvent`
+supports for specifying the size of the box.  See the API documentation for
+details.  If you do not specify any options for the box size, :meth:`addMixture`
+will attempt to find an appropriately sized box for the components given.
+
+Although :meth:`addMixture` is much more general than :meth:`addSolvent` in that
+it can pack boxes of any molecule or mixture of molecules, not just water and
+monatomic ions, it also has some limitations to be aware of.
+
+* :meth:`addMixture` does not use a :class:`ForceField`.  It instead packs the
+  mixture components by treating each copy as a rigid body and each atom as a
+  sphere.  The resulting solvent box configurations must therefore be minimized
+  with a :class:`ForceField` prior to using them for simulations.  Box sizes
+  guessed by :meth:`addMixture` may be inaccurate, and results should always be
+  equilibrated with a barostat using the desired force field before simulating.
+* Currently, ``count`` is the only available option for controlling the mixture
+  composition.  If you wish to add components at particular concentrations or in
+  a certain ratio, you should calculate an appropriate number of each component.
+* If you want to add solvent consisting of water and monatomic ions to a model,
+  you should always use :meth:`addSolvent`, as it will use a pre-equilibrated
+  water box.  The resulting configurations will require less simulation time to
+  equilibrate than the random packings generated by :meth:`addMixture`.
+* OpenMM does not provide any methods for generating conformers of components,
+  or generating topologies from, *e.g.*, SMILES strings; you must provide a
+  :class:`Topology` and positions for each one yourself. However, other packages
+  such the `OpenFF Toolkit`_ provide methods for doing this.  Their outputs can
+  be used as inputs to :meth:`addMixture`.
+
+Besides packing solvent around an existing molecule, :meth:`addMixture` can also
+be used to generate boxes containing only solvent.  Simply create a
+:class:`Modeller` with an empty :class:`Topology` and no positions, *e.g.*:
+::
+
+    modeller = Modeller(Topology(), [])
+
+and call :meth:`addMixture` on it with the desired options.
+
+.. _`OpenFF Toolkit`: https://github.com/openforcefield/openff-toolkit
+
